// example_generics_bad_param_type.mir — REJECTION CASE. A generic_param's declared type
// must be the builtin 'type' keyword, the name of a TRAIT (a bound, '[T: Hashable]'), or
// one of bool/an integer kind/usize. An enum is none of those.
//
// Since bounds were added, a NAMED type in this position is accepted on shape and then
// checked for trait-ness, so the diagnostic names the actual problem ("'Color' is not a
// trait") rather than restating the whole rule. That check runs late — after every trait is
// registered and laid out — because resolving a bound's name during the declare phase would
// force the named type's layout before symbol tables are finished.
//
// The same rule applies to a generic *function*, which used to escape it entirely:
// validate_generic_param_types documents itself as shared by all four decl kinds carrying
// generic_params (type/fn/impl/trait-impl), but declare_one_decl's FunctionDecl branch never
// called it. 'fn bad_fn[E: Color]' was therefore accepted at its declaration, and the user
// only found out at the call site via an unrelated-looking "generic argument 1 for 'bad_fn'
// must be a compile-time constant expression of type 'Color'".
//
// Expected: compilation fails with one error per bad parameter:
//   error: generic parameter 'E' is bound by 'Color', which is not a trait; a bound must
//          name a trait
//   error: generic parameter 'F' is bound by 'Color', which is not a trait; a bound must
//          name a trait

pub type Color = enum { Red Green Blue }

pub type Bad[E: Color] = struct {
    value: i32
}

pub fn bad_fn[F: Color](v: i32) -> i32 {
    return v
}

pub fn main() -> i32 {
    return 0
}
